@@s008 Covariance, correlation, causality, effect size
@@s009 Exercises 03–06 of week 5 (Grasple, paper and pencil) bring statistics into the course: how to measure whether two variables move together, why a correlation does not prove a cause, and how to say how large a difference is. Covariance returns, in more depth, in <a href="w09-covarianza-correlazione.html">week 9</a>.
@@s010 Covariance: two variables that move together
@@s011 The <b>covariance</b> measures whether two variables tend to lie above (or below) their mean <b>at the same time</b>:
@@s012 Symbol by symbol: <b>x̄</b> ("x bar") is the mean of the x, ȳ that of the y. <b>xᵢ − x̄</b> is the deviation of point i from the mean: positive if the point is above, negative if it is below. The <b>product</b> of the two deviations is positive when they agree (both above or both below), negative when they disagree. The sum collects all the points, and it is divided by n − 1 (some texts, such as the DW notes, divide by n: with many data the difference is small).
@@s013 Positive covariance: the variables grow together. Negative: when one grows the other falls. Close to zero: no <b>linear</b> tendency. Its weakness is that it depends on the units of measure, so the number alone does not say whether the relation is strong.
@@s014 Five points, five products of deviations
@@s015 Correlation: covariance without units
@@s016 To remove the units one divides the covariance by the two standard deviations. One obtains Pearson's <b>correlation coefficient</b>:
@@s017 r is a pure number, always between −1 and 1. <b>r = 1</b>: the points lie exactly on a rising line. <b>r = −1</b>: on a falling line. <b>r ≈ 0</b>: no linear tendency. Intermediate values say how narrow the cloud of points is around a line, <b>not</b> how steep the line is: an almost flat line with all the points above has r = 1.
@@s018 In NumPy: <code>np.corrcoef(x, y)[0, 1]</code>. For the link with the inner product: r is the cosine of the angle between the two vectors of deviations, x − x̄ and y − ȳ (<a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a>).
@@s019 r = 0.88, 0.05, −0.91
@@s020 Correlation sees only lines
@@s021 A frequent error: reading r = 0 as "the variables have nothing to do with each other". r measures only <b>linear</b> relations. A curved relation, even a perfect one, can give r = 0: with y = x² on points symmetric around zero, the falling part and the rising part cancel out.
@@s022 Practical rule: before calculating (or interpreting) a correlation one <b>looks at the graph</b>. And r is sensitive to outliers: a single point very far away can raise or lower it a lot (outliers, week 9).
@@s023 y = x²: perfect dependence, r = 0
@@s024 Correlation does not mean causation
@@s025 "Correlation does not imply causation". That two variables move together does not say that one causes the other. The classic example: ice cream sales and drownings are strongly correlated, but neither causes the other. Both are caused by a third variable, the heat.
@@s026 A variable that influences both measured ones is called a <b>confounder</b> (<i>confounder</i>). To prove a cause one needs <b>experiments</b> in which one variable is changed while the others are held fixed (for example by randomly assigning who receives a treatment), or analyses that take the confounders into account.
@@s027 Why it matters in ML: a model learns correlations, not causes. If a feature is only correlated with the answer (because of a confounder), the model works as long as the correlation holds and stops working when the situation changes.
@@s028 Ice cream, drownings and the heat
@@s029 Four explanations for a correlation
@@s030 Facing a correlation between A and B there are at least four possible stories, and the numbers alone do not say which is the true one:
@@s031 <b>1. A causes B.</b> <b>2. B causes A</b> (reverse causality). <b>3. A third variable C causes both</b> (confounder). <b>4. Chance</b>: a spurious correlation, born of coincidences, frequent when many pairs of variables are compared with few data.
@@s032 In the "Correlation vs Causation" exercises the typical question is precisely: given an observed correlation, which explanation is plausible, and what would be needed to exclude the others.
@@s033 The same correlation, four stories
@@s034 Effect size: how large a difference is
@@s035 When two groups are compared (a new method and an old one, one model and another) it is not enough to know <b>whether</b> there is a difference: one must know <b>how large</b> it is. A difference of 4 points is enormous if the marks vary little, negligible if they vary a lot.
@@s036 The <b>effect size</b> measures the difference in units of standard deviation. The most used is <b>Cohen's d</b>:
@@s037 where s is the combined standard deviation of the two groups (<i>pooled</i>). The conventional reading: <b>0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large</b>. The correlation r is also used as an effect size (0.1, 0.3, 0.5 as typical thresholds).
@@s038 The effect size answers "how much does the difference matter", not "how sure are we that it exists". With very few data even a large d may be due to chance; with very many data even a tiny d turns out "statistically significant" but may have no practical importance.
@@s039 Two study methods: d ≈ 0.97
